{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966785,"nonce":"1Rh4TIK9vnjITJoxieMkefeSqig=","title":"The cross-file trigger flake was two bugs - a dropped state handover and a registry that","message":"RESOLVED 2026-08-15. `cross_file_fsm_import_advances_state_via_trigger`\nfailed about one run in five, always as `Idle` where `Running` was\nexpected. Two independent causes, neither of them test noise.\n\n**The state handover.** An FSM's current state has two homes: a\n`SharedState` once `BlincContextState` exists, and a fallback table\nbefore that, which is where dispatch records. Creating the `SharedState`\nseeded it from the FSM's DECLARED INITIAL, so every transition that\nfired before the context arrived was discarded. It now adopts the\nfallback entry and takes it. Reproduced deterministically in\n`state_survives_context_init.rs`, which is the regression test.\n\n**The accumulating registry.** The DSL's `FsmRegistry` is keyed by\n`FsmId { module, type_id }`, and a recompile in the same process mints a\nfresh `TypeId` — so the same declaration gained an entry per compile.\n`find_visible` then read a name that means one FSM as ambiguous,\nreturned `None`, and the trigger call NEVER LOWERED: no dispatch, no\nerror, a silent no-op. `upsert` now replaces per `(module, name)`, the\nsame rule the runtime registry uses.\n\n**What it cost to find:** the giveaway was a probe on both dispatch and\nread showing only ONE line in a failing run — the assert's read, with no\ndispatch and no precondition read. That says the call was never in the\nprogram, which points at compile time, not at runtime state. A test that\nonly asserts the outcome cannot tell those apart.\n\nSee gotcha fsm identity needs module for the identity rules both\nregistries now share.","files":null}]}